# Cloudflare Pages reads this from dist/ (like public/_redirects).
#
# The widget is embedded in OTHER sites, so everything it loads at runtime is a
# cross-origin request from the host page's point of view. Fonts are the strict case:
# a font is always fetched in CORS mode, so a missing Access-Control-Allow-Origin
# means the self-hosted Raleway silently fails to render (issue #91).
#
# Pages already serves static assets with `access-control-allow-origin: *`; this pins
# that rather than leaving a rendering requirement on a platform default. Hashed,
# immutable build assets, so the long cache is safe — a change ships under a new URL.
# Everything Vite emits under /assets/ is content-hashed, so the long immutable cache is
# safe — a change ships under a new URL. `embed.js` deliberately sits outside this rule at
# the dist root: it is the one stable URL hosts hardcode, and it must stay revalidated.
/assets/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The two unhashed entry files at the dist root (#148). Everything above is
# content-hashed and so may be cached forever; these carry stable names precisely so hosts
# can hardcode them, which means the CDN must revalidate instead.
#
# **This rule is a fix, not a formality.** The file's previous version left `embed.js`
# outside `/assets/*` and said it "must stay revalidated" — but nothing enforced it, so the
# platform default applied, and the default differs per host: `sahajatlas.com` served
# `max-age=14400` while `*.pages.dev` served `max-age=0`. Four hours of freshness on the one
# unhashed file that imports content-hashed chunks is exactly the failure `docs/embedding.md`
# warns hosts about — a stale loader asking for chunk names the deploy no longer has, which
# kills the widget with no fallback — and it was invisible on the host every check runs
# against.
#
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate` rather than `no-store`: a conditional request that answers 304
# is cheap, and it keeps the browser cache useful while making staleness impossible. CORS is
# pinned for the same reason as the two rules around it — a `type="module"` script is fetched
# in CORS mode, so a rendering requirement should not rest on a platform default.
/auto.js
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/embed.js
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate


# The locale JSON is fetched cross-origin from every host page (src/config/i18n.ts builds
# the URL from VITE_HOST), and it needs CORS for the same reason the font does — with a
# worse failure: a blocked font degrades to the system sans, blocked locales render every
# string as its raw key. Hand-edited and unhashed, so no long cache.
/locales/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

# Resist direct indexing (issue #106). The policy and the robots.txt interaction are
# written down once, in public/robots.txt; what follows is only why this half exists.
#
# A HEADER reaches what a <meta> cannot. index.html's tag covers every ROUTE (`_redirects`
# rewrites unknown paths to that shell), but the three root entry files, the hashed assets
# and the locale JSON are each a fetchable URL with no document to carry a tag. This covers
# all of them.
#
# `/*` does not disturb the rules above: Cloudflare Pages applies EVERY matching rule
# ("an incoming request which matches multiple rules' URL patterns will inherit all rules'
# headers"), so /assets/* keeps its CORS + immutable cache and additionally gets this.
# Ordered last for reading only: these rules set disjoint header NAMES, and order
# would start to matter the moment two rules set the same one (Pages joins repeated
# values with a comma). The smoke lane pins the CORS half against exactly that.
#
# It cannot leak into a host page's own indexing: X-Robots-Tag governs the URL it is served
# on, and a directive on a subresource says nothing about the document embedding it.
# `noindex` alone, deliberately — no `nofollow`, because the canonicals point at WeMeditate
# and there is no reason to stop a crawler following them there.
/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
